#1 Diagnostics and errors

StructMark attaches source locations and diagnostics to the AST. Each diagnostic has a code, severity, message, line, and column so tools can report the cause rather than silently discard invalid content.

#2 Diagnostic structure

Read `document.diagnostics` and use workspace validation where cross-file references are involved. An empty HTML string is not a reliable validity signal.

```typescript
import {parseDocument, validateWorkspace} from "structmark-language-core";

const document = parseDocument(source);
for (const issue of document.diagnostics) {
  console.log(issue.code, issue.severity, issue.position.line, issue.message);
}
```

#2 Syntax diagnostics

Unterminated blocks and inline markers, unknown directives, and invalid attributes should produce diagnostics. The parser does not execute embedded scripts or treat raw HTML input as trusted markup.

```sm
&note[title="Unclosed block"]
This block has no terminator.
```

#2 Semantic diagnostics

Missing targets and duplicated IDs can be detected in a single document. Cross-file references must be checked in the workspace pass.

```sm
#2 Introduction (#intro)

See &ref[missing] for details.
```

#2 Lossy conversions

Not all StructMark semantics have Markdown equivalents. Use `toMarkdownDetailed()` to inspect diagnostics alongside the exported text. Highlights and underlines may report the `SMK-C002` degradation warning.

```typescript
import {parseDocument, toMarkdownDetailed} from "structmark-language-core";

const result = toMarkdownDetailed(parseDocument("=[important]"));
console.log(result.source);
console.log(result.diagnostics);
```

#2 Testing

`npm test`, `npm run fuzz`, and large-document tests cover core paths. The fuzz smoke test does not guarantee complete security or cross-browser coverage.
